(data): {\"combined_score\": 0.24671419726711147, \"correct\": true, \"search_nrmse\": 1.0275944528126153, \"search_permuted_control_gap\": 0.568478634785295, \"search_skill_vs_random_selection\": 0.13203746446411566}\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"selected_prompt_family\": \"paper_standalone\",\n  \"recommended_title\": \"How Far Do k Benchmarks Go? Cold-Start Benchmark Selection and Score Imputation on Family-Disjoint Held-Out Models\",\n  \"central_question\": \"When only k benchmarks can be run on a new model, how accurately can a history-fitted selector-plus-imputer recover its scores on the remaining benchmarks, how does that error fall with k, and how much of the gain comes from cross-benchmark structure versus the choice of which k to run?\",\n  \"central_thesis\": \"On three public leaderboards (MTEB, Open LLM Leaderboard, HELM Lite) with family-disjoint held-out models, a wrapper-selected, rank-Gauss-transformed, NNLS-stacked Gaussian imputer cuts held-out error substantially relative to column-mean imputation (final qualified outcome; skill 0.434, nRMSE 0.562) and beats permuted-observation controls by a wide margin, but its edge over random-k selection with a ridge imputer is small (0.016), error falls only with diminishing returns in k (MTEB nRMSE 0.787 at k=2 to 0.638 at k=20), and difficulty differs by leaderboard.\",\n  \"narrative_rationale\": \"The sealed final evaluation completed with outcome qualified (final.qualification.outcome = qualified, status = completed, correct = true), so the paper can report measured results as a positive-but-modest finding. The strongest defensible story is a calibrated empirical characterization of the cold-start budget question: (i) cross-benchmark structure is real and exploitable (large gain over column mean, large permuted-control gap), (ii) the marginal value of choosing the subset cleverly versus randomly is small in this regime, and (iii) error vs k shows diminishing returns on MTEB. This answers the task's 'how large must k be' question with an error-vs-k curve rather than a threshold, and avoids overclaiming selection or a mechanism: the evaluator reports selector+imputer jointly, no ablation separating selection from imputation produced numbers (ablation_studies is empty, offline experiment scripts were not executed in the evidence), no theory check results exist, and candidate diagnostics are marked unverified. Thesis-first organization (question, protocol, method, error-vs-k, baselines/controls, per-leaderboard difficulty, limitations) beats discovery order. No downstream_study block exists in paper_data.json; the search stage (three model variants, all scored) is used only as a small, clearly labelled search-partition comparison on the MTEB search split, kept separate from the sealed final results.\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"rejected\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\": \"Not in edit mode: no previous_paper or staged inputs exist. Fresh framing; nothing from a prior manuscript is preserved or rejected beyond this default.\",\n  \"staged_input_impact\": [\n    \"No staged paper, program, or edit context is present; framing is derived only from paper_data.json (final_evaluation, search-stage program records), the task contract/evaluators, and the candidate source files.\"\n  ],\n  \"method_groups\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Wrapper-selected, rank-Gauss stacked Gaussian imputer (selected configuration)\",\n      \"role\": \"proposed\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The studied method (best search score; best_correct = c02). Components: rank-Gauss or z-score representation, EM Gaussian with missing entries, benchmark selection from covariance-trace greedy and K-fold held-out-error wrapper forward selection (choice by history-only CV), per-target NNLS stack of Gaussian conditional means at several shrinkages, ridge on selected benchmarks, kNN over history models and column median, range clipping, median-shrink fallback. Described from the source file; every tuned quantity uses history rows only.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Greedy conditional-variance selection with Gaussian conditional-mean imputation\",\n      \"role\": \"ablation\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"First model variant; the only grounded comparison to the selected one under the search protocol (search nRMSE 1.067 vs 1.028; skill vs random 0.100 vs 0.132; permuted gap 0.522 vs 0.568). Differences confound transform, selector, stacking and clipping, so it must be presented as a variant comparison, not a component ablation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Monotone latent-skill member plus A-optimal latent selector added to the stack\",\n      \"role\": \"ablation\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Third variant; its search metrics and per-seed scores are identical to the selected one (combined 0.2467, both seeds), i.e. no measurable gain. Report as a null result. Cause (member not retained by CV, vs. no effect) is unknown from the artifacts; state that.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Evaluator baselines and controls: column-mean imputation, random-k ridge, greedy-forward-selection ridge, permuted and constant-observation controls\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"These define skill (1 - candidate nRMSE / baseline nRMSE, clipped to [-1,1]) and the permuted-control gap; they ground the 0.434 / 0.016 / 0.099 / 0.678 numbers. Only the aggregated values are available, not per-baseline nRMSE.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"results_focus\": [\n    \"Error-vs-k on MTEB (final): nRMSE 0.787 (k=2), 0.722 (k=5), 0.664 (k=10), 0.638 (k=20); monotone with diminishing returns; this is the direct answer to 'how large a k'. Only four k values and a single curve; no baseline curve is available, so do not claim a 'sufficient k' threshold.\",\n    \"Per-leaderboard sealed-final nRMSE: MTEB 0.703, HELM Lite 0.501, Open LLM Leaderboard 0.482, pooled 0.562 (equal weights across seeds, k grid and families). State that k grids and column counts differ by family (MTEB k in {2,5,10,20} of 297 columns; OLL k in {1,2,3} of 6 columns; HELM k in {2,3,5} of 10 columns), so cross-family differences are not a pure difficulty ranking.\",\n    \"Skill against baselines (final, pooled): vs column-mean 0.434 (three seed episodes 0.433, 0.427, 0.433), vs greedy-forward ridge 0.099, vs random-k ridge 0.016. Lead with the honest reading: large gain over history-only, modest over ridge, marginal over random selection; selector and imputer are evaluated jointly, so the 0.016 cannot be attributed to selection alone.\",\n    \"Negative-control evidence: permuted-observation control gap 0.678 (control nRMSE minus real nRMSE, pooled), supporting that gains come from cross-benchmark information in the observed scores.\",\n    \"Search-partition variant comparison (MTEB search split only, two seeds each): c01 0.2199 / nRMSE 1.067; c02 0.2467 / 1.028; c03 0.2467 / 1.028 (identical to c02). Keep clearly separate from sealed final results; skill-vs-mean on search is combined_score internally, present it as skill vs column mean.\",\n    \"Scope and limitations as a results view: held-out models are family-group-disjoint (>=25% of groups by hash order, OLL capped at 300 held-out rows); non-adaptive selection committed before any new-model score; three seeds (201-203); candidate diagnostics unverified; no confidence intervals for pooled metrics beyond seed-level final-score episodes; scores differ in meaning across leaderboards.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_plan\": [\n    \"Fig. 1 (overview, page 1-2): cold-start protocol schematic: history matrix with missing cells -> commit k columns (non-adaptive) -> observe k scores of held-out model -> imputer -> predictions on remaining N-k columns scored in column-std units; with a side strip naming the three leaderboards and the baselines/controls.\",\n    \"Fig. 2 (main results, 3 panels, full width): (a) MTEB nRMSE vs k; (b) per-leaderboard and pooled final nRMSE; (c) pooled skill against column-mean, greedy-ridge, random-k ridge. Only grounded numbers; no invented error bars (use seed-episode points only where seed values exist, i.e. final skill vs column mean).\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_specs\": [\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"protocol_overview\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Glance-value schematic of the cold-start benchmark-subset selection and imputation protocol and what is scored.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"group_by\": \"pipeline_stage\",\n      \"metric\": \"none\",\n      \"split_by\": \"none\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"ICLR template is single-column (text width about 5.5 in); a left-to-right protocol diagram with 5 labelled stages needs full text width for legible 8-9 pt labels; place on page 1 or 2.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TKEw8JaXsJLvmWoUD9gyiC","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass{article}\n2\t% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n3\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n4\t\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n5\t\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n6\t\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n7\t\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n8\t  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n9\t  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n10\t  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n11\t  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n12\t  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n13\t  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n14\t  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n15\t  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n16\t}\n17\t% --- end watermark ---\n18\t % For LaTeX2e\n19\t\\usepackage{paper,times}\n20\t\n21\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n22\t\\usepackage{url}\n23\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n24\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n25\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n26\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n27\t\n28\t\\newcommand{\\nrmse}{\\mathrm{nRMSE}}\n29\t\n30\t\\title{How Far Do $k$ Benchmarks Go? Cold-Start Benchmark Selection and Score Imputation on Family-Disjoint Held-Out Models}\n31\t\n32\t\\begin{document}\n33\t\\maketitle\n34\t\n35\t\\begin{abstract}\n36\tEvaluating a new model on every benchmark of a leaderboard is expensive, yet historical score matrices suggest that benchmarks are strongly redundant. We study the cold-start version of this problem: given a model$\\times$benchmark history with missing cells, commit to $k$ benchmarks before seeing the new model, run only those, and impute the rest. We evaluate a selector--imputer pair that combines a rank-Gauss transform, an EM-fitted Gaussian with missing data, cross-validated wrapper selection, and a per-target non-negative stack of imputers. Evaluation uses held-out models from model families disjoint from the history on three public leaderboards (MTEB, Open LLM Leaderboard, HELM Lite). The pair attains pooled normalized RMSE 0.562, a mean relative error reduction (skill) of 0.434 against column-mean imputation, and beats permuted-observation controls by a wide margin, but its skill over random-$k$ selection with a ridge imputer is only 0.016. On MTEB the error falls from 0.787 at $k{=}2$ to 0.638 at $k{=}20$ with sharply diminishing returns. Selector and imputer are scored jointly, three seeds do not resample held-out models, and no component ablation was run, so we do not attribute the gain to either part.\n37\t\\end{abstract}\n38\t\n39\t\\section{Introduction}\n40\tA full evaluation of a language or embedding model touches dozens to hundreds of benchmarks, each costing compute, time and human effort. Public leaderboards meanwhile accumulate large model$\\times$benchmark score matrices, and benchmark suites such as MMLU, BIG-bench, HELM and MTEB \\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB} are known to overlap in what they measure. This suggests a budgeted-evaluation protocol: an evaluator holding a historical matrix with $M$ models, $N$ benchmarks and missing cells receives a new model, can afford only $k\\ll N$ benchmark runs, and wants to infer the scores on the other $N-k$ benchmarks as accurately as possible. Two decisions then matter: \\emph{which} $k$ benchmarks to run, and \\emph{how} to turn their scores into predictions. A practitioner also needs to know how large $k$ must be.\n41\t\n42\tPrior work on cheaper evaluation mostly reduces the number of \\emph{items} within one benchmark \\citep{Polo2024TinyBenchmarks,Vivek2024AnchorPoints,Perlitz2024EfficientBenchmarking}, distills a handful of benchmarks into a sparse one \\citep{Kipnis2024Metabench}, or fits low-dimensional capability models across many benchmarks \\citep{Ruan2024ObservationalScaling,Polo2024Sloth}. The setting studied here differs in three ways: the selection is committed \\emph{before} any score of the new model is seen, the target is the score on every unrun benchmark, and the new model comes from a family that is absent from the history.\n43\t\n44\t\\paragraph{Research question and hypotheses.} When only $k$ benchmarks can be run on a new model, how accurately can a history-fitted selector--imputer pair recover its other scores, how does the error fall with $k$, and how much of the gain comes from cross-benchmark structure versus the choice of which $k$ to run? We examine four hypotheses.\n45\t\\textbf{H1}: observed scores on a selected subset predict the remaining scores better than history-only column means.\n46\t\\textbf{H2}: the error decreases with $k$ with diminishing returns.\n47\t\\textbf{H3}: a model-based selector adds accuracy beyond random selection under a comparable imputer.\n48\t\\textbf{H4}: difficulty differs across leaderboards.\n49\t\n50\t\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} Using a sealed evaluation on family-disjoint held-out models from three leaderboards, H1 and H2 are supported, H4 is observed but confounded by the number of benchmarks per leaderboard, and H3 is supported only weakly (a 0.016 skill margin over random-$k$ ridge). Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} summarises the protocol.\n51\t\n52\t\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n53\t(i) A precise cold-start protocol with committed selection, family-disjoint held-out models, evaluator-owned baselines and negative controls, and a hierarchical aggregation rule (Section~\\ref{sec:protocol}).\n54\t(ii) A reimplementable selector--imputer pair built from standard components: rank-Gauss transform, EM Gaussian, wrapper selection, NNLS stacking (Section~\\ref{sec:method}).\n55\t(iii) An error-versus-$k$ curve on MTEB and per-leaderboard errors, with skill relative to three baselines and a permutation control (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n56\t(iv) A calibrated account of what the evidence cannot show: no ablation, no theory check, joint scoring of selector and imputer, and seed variation that does not reflect held-out sampling (Section~\\ref{sec:limits}).\n57\t\n58\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n59\t\\centering\n60\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/protocol_overview.png}\n61\t\\caption{Cold-start protocol. A history matrix with missing cells is given to the method; it commits to $k$ benchmark indices; only the new model's scores on those $k$ benchmarks are revealed; the method predicts all other benchmarks; error is measured in units of history column standard deviations and compared with column-mean, random-$k$ ridge and greedy-forward ridge baselines and with permuted-observation controls.}\n62\t\\label{fig:overview}\n63\t\\end{figure}\n64\t\n65\t\\section{Related Work}\n66\t\\paragraph{Benchmark suites and their overlap.} Multi-task suites aggregate many sub-benchmarks \\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB,Zhuo2024BigCodeBench}, and judge-based protocols add conversational evaluations \\citep{Zheng2023MTBench}. \\citet{Perlitz2024BenchBench} show that conclusions about benchmark agreement depend strongly on methodological choices such as the reference set and the correlation measure; we therefore define the target, the held-out split and the aggregation explicitly rather than relying on a correlation summary.\n67\t\n68\t\\paragraph{Cheaper evaluation within or across benchmarks.} tinyBenchmarks, anchor points and efficient-benchmarking studies shrink the number of items of a single benchmark while preserving its score or ranking \\citep{Polo2024TinyBenchmarks,Vivek2024AnchorPoints,Perlitz2024EfficientBenchmarking}. metabench distills six benchmarks into a sparse one using the responses of thousands of models and checks recovery of the original scores \\citep{Kipnis2024Metabench}. These works reduce item counts; here the unit of selection is a whole benchmark and the target is every benchmark not run.\n69\t\n70\t\\paragraph{Latent-capability and scaling views.} Observational scaling laws and skill scaling laws explain multi-benchmark performance through a few latent capability factors estimated from public evaluations \\citep{Ruan2024ObservationalScaling,Polo2024Sloth}, and item response theory has long modelled responses through latent ability \\citep{Embretson2000IRT,Vania2021ComparingTestSets}. Our imputer uses a Gaussian dependence structure rather than an explicit latent-factor law, and we evaluate on family-disjoint models rather than within-family extrapolation.\n71\t\n72\t\\paragraph{Missing data, matrix completion and design.} Gaussian models with missing entries are fitted by EM \\citep{Dempster1977EM} under missingness assumptions discussed by \\citet{Rubin1976Inference}. Low-rank and probabilistic matrix completion \\citep{Candes2009MatrixCompletion,Salakhutdinov2007PMF} are the standard alternatives for filling score matrices. Selecting observations to minimise posterior uncertainty is an experimental-design problem \\citep{Chaloner1995BayesianDesign}; for Gaussian processes, greedy selection of informative variables has near-optimality guarantees when the objective is submodular \\citep{Nemhauser1978Submodular,Krause2007ObservationSelection,Krause2008NearOptimalSensor}. Stacked generalization combines predictors with weights fitted on held-out predictions \\citep{Wolpert1992Stacked,Breiman1996Stacked}, and ridge regression \\citep{Hoerl1970Ridge} is the linear baseline used by the evaluator.\n73\t\n74\t\\section{Method}\\label{sec:method}\n75\t\n76\t\\subsection{Problem setup and success criterion}\\label{sec:setup}\n77\tLet $X\\in\\mathbb{R}^{M\\times N}$ be a history matrix with observation mask $O\\in\\{0,1\\}^{M\\times N}$; benchmark $j$ has history mean $m_j$ and standard deviation $\\sigma_j$ computed over its observed cells. A new model $i$ has scores $y_{ij}$ on a subset $\\mathcal{O}_i$ of benchmarks (not every model was run on every benchmark). A method must (a) output a set $S$ of $k$ benchmark indices \\emph{before} seeing any new-model score (non-adaptive selection), and (b) given $y_{iS}$ (only the entries in $\\mathcal{O}_i\\cap S$), output predictions $\\hat y_{ij}$ for all $j$. Scoring uses the cells $\\mathcal{C}_i=\\mathcal{O}_i\\setminus S$. For one evaluation episode with $T$ held-out models,\n78\t\\begin{equation}\n79\t\\nrmse=\\sqrt{\\frac1T\\sum_{i=1}^{T}\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{C}_i|}\\sum_{j\\in\\mathcal{C}_i}\\Big(\\frac{\\hat y_{ij}-y_{ij}}{\\sigma_j}\\Big)^2},\n80\t\\end{equation}\n81\tso every held-out model has equal weight and every benchmark is on the scale of its history spread. A baseline $b$ yields the skill\n82\t\\begin{equation}\n83\t\\mathrm{skill}_b = \\mathrm{clip}\\big(1-\\nrmse/\\nrmse_b,\\,-1,\\,1\\big),\n84\t\\end{equation}\n85\tthe relative error reduction against that baseline, which is $0$ at parity and negative when worse.\n86\t\n87\t\\subsection{Design rationale}\\label{sec:rationale}\n88\tIf benchmark scores are jointly Gaussian with covariance $\\Sigma$ and benchmarks in $S$ are observed, the conditional mean $\\mathbb{E}[y_U\\mid y_S]$ ($U$ the unrun benchmarks) minimises mean squared error, and the expected squared error summed over $U$ equals $\\operatorname{tr}\\Sigma_{U\\mid S}$. Choosing $S$ greedily to reduce this trace (an A-optimal design) is therefore a principled selector \\emph{under the Gaussian model}. Concretely, for a row-vector of scores $y\\sim\\mathcal{N}(\\mu,\\Sigma)$ and observation noise $\\lambda\\ge 0$, the posterior covariance after observing benchmark $j$ is $R'=R-R_{\\cdot j}R_{j\\cdot}/(R_{jj}+\\lambda)$ with $R$ initialised at $\\Sigma$, and the resulting drop of the summed variance over the remaining benchmarks is $\\sum_{i\\in U,\\,i\\neq j}R_{ij}^2/(R_{jj}+\\lambda)$. A benchmark scores highly if it is strongly correlated with many others and itself has substantial variance, so redundant benchmarks are avoided once one of them has been chosen, because the update deflates their shared variance. The classical near-optimality guarantees for greedy selection \\citep{Nemhauser1978Submodular,Krause2008NearOptimalSensor} apply to submodular objectives; we do not claim that the trace criterion with noise and missing data satisfies the required conditions here. We state the Gaussian argument only as motivation: we did not check it against a controlled experiment, and real score matrices are bounded, skewed and sparsely observed, which can make the trace criterion misaligned with realised held-out error. Three design choices address this misalignment:\n89\t(1) a per-benchmark rank-Gauss transform makes marginals closer to normal, with predictions mapped back through the stored history quantiles and clipped to the observed history range;\n90\t(2) a wrapper selector scores candidate benchmarks directly by cross-validated held-out-model error, instead of the model-based trace alone;\n91\t(3) a per-target non-negative-least-squares stack \\citep{Lawson1995NNLS,Breiman1996Stacked} lets each target benchmark weight several imputers (Gaussian conditional means, ridge, nearest-neighbour models, column median), with a median-shrinkage fallback when the stack does not beat the median in cross-validation.\n92\tAll tuning uses history models only.\n93\t\n94\t\\subsection{Compared approaches}\\label{sec:variants}\n95\t\\textbf{Proposed pair (frozen for the sealed evaluation).} The selector--imputer pair of Section~\\ref{sec:impl}. \\textbf{Variant under search.} A simpler pair: z-scored columns, EM Gaussian, covariance-trace greedy selection with a coverage weighting, and the ridge-regularised Gaussian conditional mean, with shrinkage, factor rank and ridge strength tuned by history cross-validation. A third pair adds a monotone-link latent-skill member to the stack and a latent A-optimal selector candidate. The proposed pair and the simpler pair differ in several components at once (transform, selector candidates, stack, clipping), so their comparison (Table~\\ref{tab:search}) is a comparison of configurations, not an ablation. \\textbf{Evaluator baselines.} (a) \\emph{Column mean}: predict each benchmark's history mean. (b) \\emph{Random-$k$ ridge}: five random $k$-subsets (seeded from the dataset, $k$ and draw index), ridge imputer fitted on a rank-5 SVD-completed history (40 completion iterations), ridge strength chosen by leave-one-out over four values; the mean $\\nrmse$ over draws is the baseline. (c) \\emph{Greedy-forward ridge}: forward selection (up to 20 benchmarks) minimising leave-one-out ridge error on the remaining benchmarks of the completed history, with the same ridge imputer. Both ridge baselines share their imputer, so they probe selection with the imputer fixed; the proposed pair changes both selector and imputer.\n96\t\n97\t\\subsection{Selector and imputer}\\label{sec:impl}\n98\t\\paragraph{Representation and EM.} Two representations of the history are built: z-scoring (clipped at $\\pm8$) and an empirical rank-Gauss map $\\Phi^{-1}\\big((r-\\tfrac12)/n\\big)$ per benchmark (average ranks; benchmarks with fewer than eight observations or four distinct values fall back to z-scores). For each, EM fits $\\mathcal{N}(\\mu,\\Sigma)$ with missing entries, applying diagonal shrinkage $\\Sigma\\leftarrow(1-\\epsilon)\\Sigma+\\epsilon\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\Sigma)$ ($\\epsilon=0.02$) in each M-step (at most 60 iterations, tolerance $10^{-4}$, 25-s budget).\n99\t\n100\t\\paragraph{Selection.} Candidate subsets come from (i) covariance-trace greedy A-optimal selection: with $R$ the current posterior covariance, add $j$ maximising $\\sum_{i\\in U,i\\ne j}R_{ij}^2/(R_{jj}+\\lambda)$, then update $R\\leftarrow R-R_{\\cdot j}R_{j\\cdot}/(R_{jj}+\\lambda)$; and (ii) a greedy forward \\emph{wrapper} that adds the benchmark minimising five-fold held-out-model squared error of the conditional-Gaussian predictor, with all candidates scored by batched matrix products and an optional coverage weighting (benchmarks missing for many history models are less informative). For each representation and candidate rule, the subset with the lowest history cross-validation error in raw benchmark units is chosen (up to 250 candidates per step).\n101\t\n102\t\\paragraph{Imputation.} For a new model, the Gaussian conditional mean $\\mu+\\Sigma_{\\cdot S}(\\Sigma_{SS}+\\lambda I)^{-1}(y_S-\\mu_S)$ conditions only on the selected benchmarks that model actually has. Shrinkage, factor rank and $\\lambda$ are tuned by five-fold history cross-validation. The stack uses, per target benchmark, non-negative weights over six base predictors (three Gaussian conditional means at shrinkage 0, 0.15 and 0.3, ridge on the selected benchmarks, and kNN with 5 and 15 neighbours) plus an implicit column-median term, fitted on out-of-fold history predictions and regularised toward a pooled weight vector; a regularisation strength is chosen from six values by cross-validation. Outputs are mapped back to raw units, clipped to the history range, and replaced by the observed value for selected benchmarks; a model with no selected score receives the column median.\n103\t\n104\t\\paragraph{Cost and applicability.} All components are CPU-only NumPy. Fitting plus selection for one $(\\text{dataset},k)$ episode is dominated by EM and cross-validation; the sealed evaluation of 14 episodes per seed took about 157--161 seconds per seed. The method presumes a history matrix with enough coverage per benchmark and a new model whose scores are exchangeable with history models after transformation, which family-disjoint evaluation tests but does not guarantee. Several steps are guarded by wall-clock budgets (candidate evaluation after 70 s, full tuning after 150 s, stacking after 210 s), so on slow hardware the method degrades to the Gaussian-only model.\n105\t\n106\t\\subsection{Experimental protocol}\\label{sec:protocol}\n107\t\\paragraph{Data.} Sealed evaluation uses three public leaderboards at pinned revisions: MTEB results (297 benchmark columns; 124 history and 37 held-out models), the Open LLM Leaderboard table (six benchmarks; 3371 history and 300 held-out models, the held-out set capped at 300), and HELM Lite v1.13.0 core scenarios (ten scenarios; 66 history and 25 held-out models). Models are grouped by normalised name, family key and content hash (union-find); groups, never single models, are assigned to history or held-out, so no family or duplicated record appears on both sides. Held-out groups are drawn in hash order until at least a quarter of rows are held out. A separate MTEB partition with disjoint groups was used for model development (Section~\\ref{sec:search}).\n108\t\n109\t\\paragraph{Episodes.} Each seed runs 14 episodes: MTEB at $k\\in\\{2,5,10,20\\}$; Open LLM Leaderboard at $k\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$; HELM Lite at $k\\in\\{2,3,5\\}$; plus evaluator-owned controls. In a \\emph{permuted} control, held-out models' observed values on the selected benchmarks are cyclically reassigned to other held-out models (MTEB $k{=}10$, Open LLM $k{=}2$, HELM $k{=}3$); in a \\emph{constant} control the observations are replaced by column means (MTEB $k{=}10$). Controls separate genuine cross-model information from the imputer's marginal prior. The seeds (201, 202, 203) reach only the candidate's random state; the held-out sets, baselines and controls are fixed by hashing.\n110\t\n111\t\\paragraph{Why this protocol answers the question.} Four design decisions tie the measurement to the research question. (i) Family-disjoint held-out models make the new model genuinely new: if siblings of a held-out model were in the history, a nearest-neighbour effect could masquerade as cross-benchmark structure. (ii) Committing to $S$ before any new-model score is observed matches the budgeted setting in which the evaluator cannot adapt the run list, and it prevents the selector from peeking at the targets. (iii) Equal weight per held-out model, and errors in units of each benchmark's history spread, stop benchmarks with large numeric ranges or models with many observed cells from dominating the metric. (iv) Evaluator-owned baselines and permutation controls keep the comparison objects outside the control of the method: the method cannot tune its own competitors. The remaining weakness is that the held-out set is fixed, which we return to in Section~\\ref{sec:limits}.\n112\t\n113\t\\paragraph{Aggregation.} Within a leaderboard, values are averaged over seeds, then over its $k$ grid with equal weights; the pooled metric averages the three leaderboards with weight $1/3$ each. The permuted-control gap is the control $\\nrmse$ minus the matched real-episode $\\nrmse$, averaged over seeds and then over leaderboards. A run counts as valid only if all episodes complete with finite predictions, real episodes beat their controls, and pooled skill against column mean and random-$k$ ridge is positive. The sealed evaluation finished with outcome \\emph{qualified}. This statement concerns the evaluated setting; no further qualification diagnostics were retained.\n114\t\n115\t\\section{Results}\\label{sec:results}\n116\t\n117\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n118\t\\centering\n119\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/main_results.pdf}\n120\t\\caption{Sealed evaluation on family-disjoint held-out models. (a) MTEB $\\nrmse$ against budget $k$ (mean over three seeds). (b) $\\nrmse$ per leaderboard (OLL: Open LLM Leaderboard) and pooled over the three. (c) Skill against column-mean, greedy-forward ridge and random-$k$ ridge baselines, pooled over leaderboards. No error bars: the retained metrics are seed means, and the three seeds share the same held-out models.}\n121\t\\label{fig:main}\n122\t\\end{figure}\n123\t\n124\t\\subsection{Main results}\n125\tPooled over the three leaderboards, the proposed pair reaches $\\nrmse=0.562$ and a skill of 0.434 against column-mean imputation, that is, a mean relative error reduction of 43.4\\% (Table~\\ref{tab:final}). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_score]]\n126\tThe three per-seed pooled skills are 0.433, 0.427 and 0.433, so the stochasticity of the method's own cross-validation folds moves the headline by less than 0.006. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.evidence.episodes]]\n127\tThis supports H1 in the evaluated setting. Because all three seeds use identical held-out models, this spread does not measure how the result would change with different held-out models, and we report no confidence interval for the pooled values.\n128\t\n129\t\\begin{table}[t]\n130\t\\centering\n131\t\\caption{Sealed final metrics of the frozen pair (mean over seeds 201--203; hierarchy described in Section~\\ref{sec:protocol}). Lower $\\nrmse$ is better; skill is relative error reduction against the named baseline.}\n132\t\\label{tab:final}\n133\t\\small\n134\t\\begin{tabular}{lcl}\n135\t\\toprule\n136\tMetric & Value & Meaning\\\\\n137\t\\midrule\n138\tPooled $\\nrmse$ & 0.562 & mean over 3 leaderboards\\\\\n139\t\\quad MTEB / HELM / Open LLM & 0.703 / 0.501 / 0.482 & per leaderboard\\\\\n140\t\\quad MTEB $k{=}2/5/10/20$ & 0.787/0.722/0.664/0.638 & error vs.\\ budget\\\\\n141\tSkill vs.\\ column mean & 0.434 & H1\\\\\n142\tSkill vs.\\ greedy-forward ridge & 0.099 & selector+imputer jointly\\\\\n143\tSkill vs.\\ random-$k$ ridge & 0.016 & selector+imputer jointly\\\\\n144\tPermuted-control gap (nRMSE) & 0.678 & control minus real\\\\\n145\t\\bottomrule\n146\t\\end{tabular}\n147\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_helm]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_oll]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_greedy_ridge]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_random_selection]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_permuted_control_gap]]\n148\t\\end{table}\n149\t\n150\t\\subsection{Error against the budget $k$}\n151\tOn MTEB, $\\nrmse$ falls monotonically from 0.787 at $k{=}2$ to 0.722 at $k{=}5$, 0.664 at $k{=}10$ and 0.638 at $k{=}20$ (Figure~\\ref{fig:main}a). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb_k2]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb_k5]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb_k10]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb_k20]]\n152\tThe mean reduction per additional benchmark shrinks from about 0.022 between $k{=}2$ and $5$, to 0.012 between 5 and 10, to 0.003 between 10 and 20 (differences of the four tabulated means divided by the number of added benchmarks). This supports the diminishing-returns part of H2 on MTEB, with four budgets and no uncertainty estimate for each point. Even at $k{=}20$, i.e.\\ about 7\\% of the 297 columns, the typical per-cell error is still about 0.64 history standard deviations. We cannot say what $k$ is ``enough'': that depends on an error tolerance we did not define, and the curve has no baseline at each $k$ (the retained metrics report baseline skill only pooled over budgets). The curve is measured for one leaderboard; the Open LLM Leaderboard and HELM Lite grids ($k\\le3$ of 6 and $k\\le5$ of 10) were not retained as per-$k$ curves.\n153\t\n154\t\\subsection{Baselines and controls}\n155\t\\paragraph{Control.} The permuted-observation control raises $\\nrmse$ by 0.678 on average over the matched real episodes (Table~\\ref{tab:final}). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_permuted_control_gap]]\n156\tHence the predictions depend on the new model's own scores on the selected benchmarks rather than on a marginal prior alone. The gap is an absolute $\\nrmse$ difference and should be read against the pooled error of 0.562.\n157\t\n158\t\\paragraph{Selection versus imputation.} Skill against random-$k$ ridge is only 0.016: at the pooled level the proposed pair has a mean relative error 1.6\\% lower than five random subsets imputed by a ridge model on a completed history. Skill against greedy-forward ridge is 0.099. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_random_selection]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_greedy_ridge]]\n159\tBoth figures mix selector and imputer, so neither isolates ``which $k$''. The two baselines share an imputer and differ only in selection; since skill against greedy ridge exceeds skill against random-$k$ ridge by 0.083, the greedy-forward baseline had larger error on average than the random-$k$ baseline in these evaluations. This is an inference from two averaged skills (the per-baseline errors were not retained), and it is the only retained evidence about selection alone: under a fixed ridge imputer, a leave-one-out greedy selector did not beat random subsets. For H3 the grounded conclusion is therefore modest. The pair's accuracy over a ridge imputer does not separate into a selector effect and an imputer effect, and the small margin over random-$k$ suggests that on these leaderboards most of the achievable gain over column means is available with a reasonable imputer and an arbitrary subset.\n160\t\n161\t\\subsection{Difficulty across leaderboards}\n162\t$\\nrmse$ is 0.703 on MTEB, 0.501 on HELM Lite and 0.482 on the Open LLM Leaderboard (Figure~\\ref{fig:main}b), so H4 holds in the sense that error differs. It is not a clean difficulty ranking: the leaderboards differ in the number of benchmarks (297, 10, 6), history size (124, 66, 3371 models), $k$ grids, and what $k$ means as a fraction of the columns. MTEB lists embedding models over many task types, which is also a different object from generative language models; none of the three results transfers to the others. % @evidence[[analysis/protocol_facts.json#final]]\n163\t\n164\t\\subsection{Reading the metrics: sensitivity and failure analysis}\n165\t\\paragraph{Skill is a mean of ratios.} Each skill value is computed per episode and then averaged along the hierarchy of Section~\\ref{sec:protocol}, and is clipped to $[-1,1]$. It is therefore not equal to one minus the ratio of pooled errors. If it were, the two ridge-baseline skills would imply that the greedy-forward baseline has an error about $(1-0.016)/(1-0.099)\\approx 1.09$ times that of the random-$k$ baseline. We read this only as indicative of the sign and rough size of the effect. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_random_selection]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_greedy_ridge]]\n166\t\\paragraph{Where the pair could fail.} Three mechanisms can degrade the predictions without being visible in the pooled numbers: (a) benchmarks with few history observations fall back to z-scores or to coarse medians, and the clipped outputs cannot extrapolate beyond the history range, so a new model that outperforms every history model on a benchmark is necessarily under-predicted on that benchmark; (b) selected benchmarks that a new model has not been run on contribute nothing, and the pair then falls back to a prior that conditions on fewer scores (a model with none of the selected scores receives column medians); (c) the wall-clock guards switch off the stack or optional candidates on slow hardware. We did not construct stress tests for (a)--(c), so we cannot report how often or by how much they matter.\n167\t\\paragraph{What a different outcome would have looked like.} A permuted-control gap near zero would have meant that the predictions were driven by the marginal prior; a negative skill against column means would have refuted H1; a flat MTEB curve would have refuted H2. The first two did not occur and the third is contradicted by the monotone curve, so the study could have refuted them. By contrast, H3 and H4 are not tested sharply: the sealed metrics do not isolate selection, and the leaderboards are not matched in size.\n168\t\n169\t\\subsection{Configuration comparison on the model-development partition}\\label{sec:search}\n170\tDuring development, three configurations were scored on a separate MTEB partition (disjoint groups, 280 columns, 131 history and 32 held-out models) with two seeds and $k\\in\\{2,5,10,20\\}$. Table~\\ref{tab:search} keeps these numbers apart from the sealed ones; $\\nrmse$ values are not comparable across the two partitions.\n171\t% @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#01]] @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#02]] @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#03]]\n172\t\n173\t\\begin{table}[t]\n174\t\\centering\n175\t\\caption{Development-partition comparison (MTEB, two seeds). Score is the mean skill against column mean. The seed columns give the two per-seed scores.}\n176\t\\label{tab:search}\n177\t\\small\n178\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n179\t\\toprule\n180\tConfiguration & Score & Seeds 11/12 & $\\nrmse$ & vs.\\ random-$k$ & Perm.\\ gap\\\\\n181\t\\midrule\n182\tSimple pair (trace + cond.\\ mean) & 0.220 & 0.220 / 0.220 & 1.067 & 0.100 & 0.522\\\\\n183\tProposed pair (wrapper + stack) & 0.247 & 0.243 / 0.251 & 1.028 & 0.132 & 0.568\\\\\n184\tProposed + latent-skill member & 0.247 & 0.243 / 0.251 & 1.028 & 0.132 & 0.568\\\\\n185\t\\bottomrule\n186\t\\end{tabular}\n187\t\\end{table}\n188\t\n189\tThe proposed pair scores 0.247 against 0.220 for the simple pair, with non-overlapping per-seed ranges (0.243--0.251 vs.\\ 0.220), and also shows a larger skill over random-$k$ selection (0.132 vs.\\ 0.100). % @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#02]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#result.top_program_01]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#result.top_program_03]]\n190\tBecause several components change together and the two seeds share one held-out set, this licenses only the statement that the richer configuration was better on that partition; it does not attribute the gain to the transform, the wrapper selector or the stack. The pair extended with a latent-skill member and a latent A-optimal selector produced the same four metrics as the proposed pair to all reported digits. The extension is kept only if it improves cross-validation error, which is consistent with it being inactive, but no diagnostic recording the choice was retained, so the cause is unknown, and we treat this configuration as a null result. The sealed evaluation was run on the best-scoring configuration; the exact tie means the retained scores alone cannot distinguish the two sources (Supplementary Information).\n191\t\n192\t\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\\label{sec:limits}\n193\t\\paragraph{What is and is not shown.} The sealed result supports a bounded statement: on family-disjoint held-out models from three pinned leaderboards, a history-fitted selector--imputer pair predicts unrun benchmark scores with a mean relative error reduction (skill) of 0.434 against column means, and the improvement is not an artefact of the imputer's marginals since permuting the observations erases it. The evidence does not show that cross-benchmark structure is \\emph{exploited by selection}; it shows that observed scores help, and that a random subset with a ridge imputer comes within a skill of 0.016 of the pair.\n194\t\n195\t\\paragraph{Joint scoring and missing ablations.} Selector and imputer are scored together. No controlled experiment varied one while holding the other fixed, so the contributions of the rank-Gauss transform, the wrapper selector, the stack and the clipping are unattributed. The development-partition comparison changes many components at once. A fair selector test would pair each selector with each imputer; we did not run it.\n196\t\n197\t\\paragraph{Uncertainty.} Reported values are seed means. The seeds vary only the method's internal randomness (fold assignments), not the held-out models, baselines or controls, so the 0.006 spread across seeds understates sampling uncertainty over models. Held-out sets are small for MTEB (37 models) and HELM Lite (25), and the held-out groups are few (25 and 7). The 0.016 margin over random-$k$ should be viewed as indistinguishable from zero until a resampling-based interval over held-out models is available; we did not compute one, and we make no significance claim.\n198\t\n199\t\\paragraph{Scope.} Three leaderboards, of which two contain generative language models in small numbers of benchmarks (six and ten) and one contains text-embedding models; scales differ (accuracies, win rates, F1). Family-disjoint splitting was defined by name and content heuristics. Selection is non-adaptive; adaptive stopping or sequential selection could change the picture. Missingness in leaderboards is not random (strong models are run on more benchmarks), which violates the usual missing-at-random assumption of EM-based imputation \\citep{Rubin1976Inference}. The run-time guards in Section~\\ref{sec:impl} make behaviour hardware-dependent.\n200\t\n201\t\\paragraph{Unperformed analyses.} We did not run a theory check of the trace objective, a component ablation, per-family error breakdowns, comparisons with low-rank matrix completion or latent-factor imputers as full alternatives, or other leaderboards. These are not claimed.\n202\t\n203\t\\paragraph{Threats to validity.} (1) \\emph{Leakage through naming.} Family-disjointness relies on name normalisation, a family key and a content hash; a model re-released under an unrecognised name could cross the split. (2) \\emph{Metric scale.} Errors are in history standard deviations of each benchmark, which makes a benchmark with a narrow history spread (e.g.\\ one saturated by strong models) look harder to predict than one with a wide spread. (3) \\emph{Heterogeneous coverage.} Leaderboard missingness is related to model strength and recency; selected benchmarks that are well covered in the history may be missing for new models. (4) \\emph{Configuration freezing.} The pair was chosen on the development partition and then evaluated once on the sealed partitions; the sealed numbers are not selected on, but the development partition is from the same MTEB snapshot, so the MTEB result is closer to the development distribution than the other two leaderboards, which were not used to choose among configurations. (5) \\emph{Implementation coupling.} The result is for one concrete implementation, including its tuning grids and wall-clock guards; a different implementation of the same design could behave differently.\n204\t\n205\t\\paragraph{Practical reading.} For an evaluator with a comparable history, a handful of well-covered benchmarks and a reasonable imputer already remove a large part of the column-mean error, further benchmarks buy progressively less, and an unavoidable residual of about 0.64 history standard deviations remains on MTEB at $k{=}20$.\n206\t\n207\t\\section{Conclusion}\n208\tWe posed the cold-start question of how well $k$ benchmark runs predict the remaining scores of a model from an unseen family, and evaluated a rank-Gauss, EM-Gaussian, wrapper-selected, NNLS-stacked pair on three leaderboards with family-disjoint held-out models. The sealed evaluation completed and qualified. Pooled nRMSE is 0.562 and skill against column means is 0.434, with a clear gap to permuted controls; on MTEB the error decreases from 0.787 to 0.638 as $k$ rises from 2 to 20 with sharply diminishing returns, and error levels differ across leaderboards. The margin over random-$k$ selection with a ridge imputer is only 0.016 and cannot be assigned to selection or imputation, and the retained evidence suggests that a leave-one-out greedy selector can fail to beat random subsets. The main unresolved questions are the isolated value of selection, uncertainty over held-out models, and behaviour beyond these three leaderboards; each needs additional controlled experiments that this study did not perform.\n209\t\n210\t\\clearpage\n211\t\\bibliography{references}\n212\t\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n213\t\n214\t\\clearpage\n215\t\\appendix\n216\t\\section{Supplementary Information}\n217\t\n218\t\\subsection{Evaluation protocol details}\n219\tTable~\\ref{tab:data} lists the data partitions used by the sealed evaluation, taken from the evaluator's dataset manifests. % @evidence[[analysis/protocol_facts.json#final]] @evidence[[scripts/extract_protocol_facts.py]]\n220\tGroup counts are numbers of family groups after union-find merging. The model-development partition (MTEB, 280 columns, 131 history models, 32 held-out models, revision identical to the sealed MTEB revision) uses a disjoint set of groups. % @evidence[[analysis/protocol_facts.json#search]]\n221\t\n222\t\\begin{table}[h]\n223\t\\centering\n224\t\\caption{Sealed evaluation partitions.}\n225\t\\label{tab:data}\n226\t\\small\n227\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{4pt}\n228\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n229\t\\toprule\n230\tLeaderboard & Revision & Cols. & History & Held-out & Groups (h/o) & $k$ grid\\\\\n231\t\\midrule\n232\tMTEB results & 6dabbcc & 297 & 124 & 37 & 92 / 25 & 2, 5, 10, 20\\\\\n233\tOpen LLM Leaderboard & 9c09a7c & 6 & 3371 & 300 & 2314 / 266 & 1, 2, 3\\\\\n234\tHELM Lite & v1.13.0 & 10 & 66 & 25 & 16 / 7 & 2, 3, 5\\\\\n235\t\\bottomrule\n236\t\\end{tabular}\n237\t\\end{table}\n238\t\n239\t\\noindent Score definitions: MTEB uses each task's main score averaged over the entries of the first available split (test, validation, dev, train), taken from the model revision directory with the most task files; Open LLM Leaderboard uses raw scores of IFEval, BBH, MATH Level 5, GPQA, MUSR and MMLU-PRO, dropping rows with a missing score; HELM Lite uses the ten scenario columns of the core-scenarios table, excluding the mean-win-rate column. Held-out MTEB and HELM Lite models may lack some scores; those cells are not scored. Per-seed pooled scores of the sealed evaluation were 0.4330 (seed 201), 0.4273 (202) and 0.4328 (203), taking 157.4, 160.6 and 161.0 seconds for 14 episodes each. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.evidence.episodes]]\n240\t\n241\t\\subsection{Method specification and hyperparameters}\n242\tTable~\\ref{tab:hyper} collects the constants of the pair described in Section~\\ref{sec:impl}; they are read from the entry-point source of the evaluated configuration (\\texttt{src/program.py}: the EM, greedy-selection, wrapper-selection, stacking and prediction routines). % @evidence[[source_evidence/config_proposed/program.py]]\n243\t\n244\t\\begin{table}[h]\n245\t\\centering\n246\t\\caption{Constants of the proposed pair.}\n247\t\\label{tab:hyper}\n248\t\\small\n249\t\\begin{tabular}{lp{0.5\\textwidth}}\n250\t\\toprule\n251\tComponent & Setting\\\\\n252\t\\midrule\n253\tEM & shrinkage 0.02, $\\le 60$ iterations, tol.\\ $10^{-4}$, jitter $10^{-4}$, 25-s budget\\\\\n254\tCross-validation & 5 folds over history models\\\\\n255\tCovariance shrinkage grid & 0, 0.05, 0.15, 0.3\\\\\n256\tFactor-rank grid & 2, 4, 6, 10, full\\\\\n257\tRidge $\\lambda$ grid (Gaussian conditional mean) & 0.003, 0.03, 0.1, 0.3\\\\\n258\tSelection candidates & 4 trace-greedy settings, 2 wrapper settings (coverage exponent 0 or 1)\\\\\n259\tWrapper & $\\lambda=0.1$, shrinkage 0.05, $\\le 250$ candidates per step\\\\\n260\tStack base predictors & Gaussian conditional means (shrink 0, 0.15, 0.3), ridge ($\\lambda=5$), kNN (5, 15)\\\\\n261\tStack regularisation grid & 0, 0.05, 0.25, 1, 5, $10^{5}$\\\\\n262\tClipping & z-scores at $\\pm 8$; outputs to the history range\\\\\n263\tWall-clock guards & optional candidates 70 s; full tuning 150 s; stacking 210 s\\\\\n264\t\\bottomrule\n265\t\\end{tabular}\n266\t\\end{table}\n267\t\n268\t\\noindent The latent-skill extension (third row of Table~\\ref{tab:search}) adds a low-rank model $T_{ij}\\approx g_j(u_i\\cdot v_j+b_j)$ with a monotone piecewise-linear link $g_j$ per benchmark, Huber-likelihood MAP inference of a new model's latent vector with a Laplace covariance, an additional stack member retained only if cross-validation improves, and a latent A-optimal selector candidate. Its metrics equal those of the proposed pair (Table~\\ref{tab:search}); the extension took roughly 331--334 seconds per search seed against 141--142 seconds for the proposed pair. % @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#03]]\n269\tThe longer run time indicates that the extra code ran, but its influence on outputs is not established. The final-evaluation record lists a hash of the evaluated source tree; we were not able to match this hash to a single source directory, and the tie above means the retained scores cannot discriminate the two. The description in the main text is of the shared stack, and the extension is described here for completeness. The simple pair used 95--97 seconds per search seed. % @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#01]]\n270\t\n271\t\\subsection{Extra evidence tables}\n272\t\\begin{table}[h]\n273\t\\centering\n274\t\\caption{Development-partition per-seed scores and durations (seconds, two seeds each).}\n275\t\\label{tab:seeds}\n276\t\\small\n277\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n278\t\\toprule\n279\tConfiguration & Score (seed 11) & Score (seed 12) & Time (seed 11) & Time (seed 12)\\\\\n280\t\\midrule\n281\tSimple pair & 0.2201 & 0.2197 & 95.4 & 97.3\\\\\n282\tProposed pair & 0.2429 & 0.2506 & 140.9 & 142.2\\\\\n283\tProposed + latent member & 0.2429 & 0.2506 & 331.2 & 334.3\\\\\n284\t\\bottomrule\n285\t\\end{tabular}\n286\t% @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json]]\n287\t\\end{table}\n288\t\n289\t\\subsection{Unperformed analyses}\n290\tThe following were not run and are not claimed: component ablations (the ablation directory of the run is missing); empirical verification of the trace objective as expected error; bootstrap or model-resampling intervals; per-leaderboard $k$ curves for Open LLM Leaderboard and HELM Lite as retained metrics; baseline $\\nrmse$ values per budget; additional leaderboards such as LiveBench, BigCodeBench or AlpacaEval, which the task description lists as optional.\n291\t\n292\t\\subsection{Reproducibility, data and licences}\n293\tEntry point: a single Python file exposing a \\texttt{handle} request function (operations \\texttt{reset}, \\texttt{load\\_history}, \\texttt{select}, \\texttt{predict}); dependencies are NumPy only; behaviour is deterministic given the seed except for the wall-clock guards. Figures and tables in this paper come from \\texttt{scripts/build\\_figures.py} (Figure~\\ref{fig:main}), \\texttt{scripts/extract\\_protocol\\_facts.py} (Table~\\ref{tab:data}), \\texttt{scripts/extract\\_search\\_metrics.py} (Tables~\\ref{tab:search} and~\\ref{tab:seeds}); the sealed metrics are read from the evaluation registry. Data sources are public: MTEB results (CC0-1.0), Open LLM Leaderboard contents (no dataset licence declared on its card), and HELM Lite release v1.13.0 (scenario data under per-scenario licences, not individually verified). Literature retrieval used only titles from the starter literature and standard methodology references; no material from the excluded publication was knowingly accessed.\n294\t\n295\t\\subsection{LLM Usage}\n296\tLarge language models were used for research hypothesis generation, for implementing and running the compared configurations, and for drafting and editing this manuscript. The selector--imputer designs, code, experiments and the text were produced by an automated LLM-driven research workflow, with evaluator-owned scoring that the workflow could not modify. All reported numbers were read from evaluator outputs and checked against the evidence registry; the human role was limited to specifying the task and evaluation setting. References were retrieved from bibliographic databases and checked against their records.\n297\t\n298\t\\end{document}\n299\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8e1be07f-12b0-4a35-9573-dafb876efa2b","uuid":"907132da-4b2d-47f2-818d-3c6b1d52ea00","timestamp":"2026-11-11T12:42:34.523Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n% --- end watermark ---\n % For LaTeX2e\n\\usepackage{paper,times}\n\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{url}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\n\\newcommand{\\nrmse}{\\mathrm{nRMSE}}\n\n\\title{How Far Do $k$ Benchmarks Go? Cold-Start Benchmark Selection and Score Imputation on Family-Disjoint Held-Out Models}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nEvaluating a new model on every benchmark of a leaderboard is expensive, yet historical score matrices suggest that benchmarks are strongly redundant. We study the cold-start version of this problem: given a model$\\times$benchmark history with missing cells, commit to $k$ benchmarks before seeing the new model, run only those, and impute the rest. We evaluate a selector--imputer pair that combines a rank-Gauss transform, an EM-fitted Gaussian with missing data, cross-validated wrapper selection, and a per-target non-negative stack of imputers. Evaluation uses held-out models from model families disjoint from the history on three public leaderboards (MTEB, Open LLM Leaderboard, HELM Lite). The pair attains pooled normalized RMSE 0.562, a mean relative error reduction (skill) of 0.434 against column-mean imputation, and beats permuted-observation controls by a wide margin, but its skill over random-$k$ selection with a ridge imputer is only 0.016. On MTEB the error falls from 0.787 at $k{=}2$ to 0.638 at $k{=}20$ with sharply diminishing returns. Selector and imputer are scored jointly, three seeds do not resample held-out models, and no component ablation was run, so we do not attribute the gain to either part.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nA full evaluation of a language or embedding model touches dozens to hundreds of benchmarks, each costing compute, time and human effort. Public leaderboards meanwhile accumulate large model$\\times$benchmark score matrices, and benchmark suites such as MMLU, BIG-bench, HELM and MTEB \\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB} are known to overlap in what they measure. This suggests a budgeted-evaluation protocol: an evaluator holding a historical matrix with $M$ models, $N$ benchmarks and missing cells receives a new model, can afford only $k\\ll N$ benchmark runs, and wants to infer the scores on the other $N-k$ benchmarks as accurately as possible. Two decisions then matter: \\emph{which} $k$ benchmarks to run, and \\emph{how} to turn their scores into predictions. A practitioner also needs to know how large $k$ must be.\n\nPrior work on cheaper evaluation mostly reduces the number of \\emph{items} within one benchmark \\citep{Polo2024TinyBenchmarks,Vivek2024AnchorPoints,Perlitz2024EfficientBenchmarking}, distills a handful of benchmarks into a sparse one \\citep{Kipnis2024Metabench}, or fits low-dimensional capability models across many benchmarks \\citep{Ruan2024ObservationalScaling,Polo2024Sloth}. The setting studied here differs in three ways: the selection is committed \\emph{before} any score of the new model is seen, the target is the score on every unrun benchmark, and the new model comes from a family that is absent from the history.\n\n\\paragraph{Research question and hypotheses.} When only $k$ benchmarks can be run on a new model, how accurately can a history-fitted selector--imputer pair recover its other scores, how does the error fall with $k$, and how much of the gain comes from cross-benchmark structure versus the choice of which $k$ to run? We examine four hypotheses.\n\\textbf{H1}: observed scores on a selected subset predict the remaining scores better than history-only column means.\n\\textbf{H2}: the error decreases with $k$ with diminishing returns.\n\\textbf{H3}: a model-based selector adds accuracy beyond random selection under a comparable imputer.\n\\textbf{H4}: difficulty differs across leaderboards.\n\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} Using a sealed evaluation on family-disjoint held-out models from three leaderboards, H1 and H2 are supported, H4 is observed but confounded by the number of benchmarks per leaderboard, and H3 is supported only weakly (a 0.016 skill margin over random-$k$ ridge). Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} summarises the protocol.\n\n\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n(i) A precise cold-start protocol with committed selection, family-disjoint held-out models, evaluator-owned baselines and negative controls, and a hierarchical aggregation rule (Section~\\ref{sec:protocol}).\n(ii) A reimplementable selector--imputer pair built from standard components: rank-Gauss transform, EM Gaussian, wrapper selection, NNLS stacking (Section~\\ref{sec:method}).\n(iii) An error-versus-$k$ curve on MTEB and per-leaderboard errors, with skill relative to three baselines and a permutation control (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n(iv) A calibrated account of what the evidence cannot show: no ablation, no theory check, joint scoring of selector and imputer, and seed variation that does not reflect held-out sampling (Section~\\ref{sec:limits}).\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/protocol_overview.png}\n\\caption{Cold-start protocol. A history matrix with missing cells is given to the method; it commits to $k$ benchmark indices; only the new model's scores on those $k$ benchmarks are revealed; the method predicts all other benchmarks; error is measured in units of history column standard deviations and compared with column-mean, random-$k$ ridge and greedy-forward ridge baselines and with permuted-observation controls.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Benchmark suites and their overlap.} Multi-task suites aggregate many sub-benchmarks \\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB,Zhuo2024BigCodeBench}, and judge-based protocols add conversational evaluations \\citep{Zheng2023MTBench}. \\citet{Perlitz2024BenchBench} show that conclusions about benchmark agreement depend strongly on methodological choices such as the reference set and the correlation measure; we therefore define the target, the held-out split and the aggregation explicitly rather than relying on a correlation summary.\n\n\\paragraph{Cheaper evaluation within or across benchmarks.} tinyBenchmarks, anchor points and efficient-benchmarking studies shrink the number of items of a single benchmark while preserving its score or ranking \\citep{Polo2024TinyBenchmarks,Vivek2024AnchorPoints,Perlitz2024EfficientBenchmarking}. metabench distills six benchmarks into a sparse one using the responses of thousands of models and checks recovery of the original scores \\citep{Kipnis2024Metabench}. These works reduce item counts; here the unit of selection is a whole benchmark and the target is every benchmark not run.\n\n\\paragraph{Latent-capability and scaling views.} Observational scaling laws and skill scaling laws explain multi-benchmark performance through a few latent capability factors estimated from public evaluations \\citep{Ruan2024ObservationalScaling,Polo2024Sloth}, and item response theory has long modelled responses through latent ability \\citep{Embretson2000IRT,Vania2021ComparingTestSets}. Our imputer uses a Gaussian dependence structure rather than an explicit latent-factor law, and we evaluate on family-disjoint models rather than within-family extrapolation.\n\n\\paragraph{Missing data, matrix completion and design.} Gaussian models with missing entries are fitted by EM \\citep{Dempster1977EM} under missingness assumptions discussed by \\citet{Rubin1976Inference}. Low-rank and probabilistic matrix completion \\citep{Candes2009MatrixCompletion,Salakhutdinov2007PMF} are the standard alternatives for filling score matrices. Selecting observations to minimise posterior uncertainty is an experimental-design problem \\citep{Chaloner1995BayesianDesign}; for Gaussian processes, greedy selection of informative variables has near-optimality guarantees when the objective is submodular \\citep{Nemhauser1978Submodular,Krause2007ObservationSelection,Krause2008NearOptimalSensor}. Stacked generalization combines predictors with weights fitted on held-out predictions \\citep{Wolpert1992Stacked,Breiman1996Stacked}, and ridge regression \\citep{Hoerl1970Ridge} is the linear baseline used by the evaluator.\n\n\\section{Method}\\label{sec:method}\n\n\\subsection{Problem setup and success criterion}\\label{sec:setup}\nLet $X\\in\\mathbb{R}^{M\\times N}$ be a history matrix with observation mask $O\\in\\{0,1\\}^{M\\times N}$; benchmark $j$ has history mean $m_j$ and standard deviation $\\sigma_j$ computed over its observed cells. A new model $i$ has scores $y_{ij}$ on a subset $\\mathcal{O}_i$ of benchmarks (not every model was run on every benchmark). A method must (a) output a set $S$ of $k$ benchmark indices \\emph{before} seeing any new-model score (non-adaptive selection), and (b) given $y_{iS}$ (only the entries in $\\mathcal{O}_i\\cap S$), output predictions $\\hat y_{ij}$ for all $j$. Scoring uses the cells $\\mathcal{C}_i=\\mathcal{O}_i\\setminus S$. For one evaluation episode with $T$ held-out models,\n\\begin{equation}\n\\nrmse=\\sqrt{\\frac1T\\sum_{i=1}^{T}\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{C}_i|}\\sum_{j\\in\\mathcal{C}_i}\\Big(\\frac{\\hat y_{ij}-y_{ij}}{\\sigma_j}\\Big)^2},\n\\end{equation}\nso every held-out model has equal weight and every benchmark is on the scale of its history spread. A baseline $b$ yields the skill\n\\begin{equation}\n\\mathrm{skill}_b = \\mathrm{clip}\\big(1-\\nrmse/\\nrmse_b,\\,-1,\\,1\\big),\n\\end{equation}\nthe relative error reduction against that baseline, which is $0$ at parity and negative when worse.\n\n\\subsection{Design rationale}\\label{sec:rationale}\nIf benchmark scores are jointly Gaussian with covariance $\\Sigma$ and benchmarks in $S$ are observed, the conditional mean $\\mathbb{E}[y_U\\mid y_S]$ ($U$ the unrun benchmarks) minimises mean squared error, and the expected squared error summed over $U$ equals $\\operatorname{tr}\\Sigma_{U\\mid S}$. Choosing $S$ greedily to reduce this trace (an A-optimal design) is therefore a principled selector \\emph{under the Gaussian model}. Concretely, for a row-vector of scores $y\\sim\\mathcal{N}(\\mu,\\Sigma)$ and observation noise $\\lambda\\ge 0$, the posterior covariance after observing benchmark $j$ is $R'=R-R_{\\cdot j}R_{j\\cdot}/(R_{jj}+\\lambda)$ with $R$ initialised at $\\Sigma$, and the resulting drop of the summed variance over the remaining benchmarks is $\\sum_{i\\in U,\\,i\\neq j}R_{ij}^2/(R_{jj}+\\lambda)$. A benchmark scores highly if it is strongly correlated with many others and itself has substantial variance, so redundant benchmarks are avoided once one of them has been chosen, because the update deflates their shared variance. The classical near-optimality guarantees for greedy selection \\citep{Nemhauser1978Submodular,Krause2008NearOptimalSensor} apply to submodular objectives; we do not claim that the trace criterion with noise and missing data satisfies the required conditions here. We state the Gaussian argument only as motivation: we did not check it against a controlled experiment, and real score matrices are bounded, skewed and sparsely observed, which can make the trace criterion misaligned with realised held-out error. Three design choices address this misalignment:\n(1) a per-benchmark rank-Gauss transform makes marginals closer to normal, with predictions mapped back through the stored history quantiles and clipped to the observed history range;\n(2) a wrapper selector scores candidate benchmarks directly by cross-validated held-out-model error, instead of the model-based trace alone;\n(3) a per-target non-negative-least-squares stack \\citep{Lawson1995NNLS,Breiman1996Stacked} lets each target benchmark weight several imputers (Gaussian conditional means, ridge, nearest-neighbour models, column median), with a median-shrinkage fallback when the stack does not beat the median in cross-validation.\nAll tuning uses history models only.\n\n\\subsection{Compared approaches}\\label{sec:variants}\n\\textbf{Proposed pair (frozen for the sealed evaluation).} The selector--imputer pair of Section~\\ref{sec:impl}. \\textbf{Variant under search.} A simpler pair: z-scored columns, EM Gaussian, covariance-trace greedy selection with a coverage weighting, and the ridge-regularised Gaussian conditional mean, with shrinkage, factor rank and ridge strength tuned by history cross-validation. A third pair adds a monotone-link latent-skill member to the stack and a latent A-optimal selector candidate. The proposed pair and the simpler pair differ in several components at once (transform, selector candidates, stack, clipping), so their comparison (Table~\\ref{tab:search}) is a comparison of configurations, not an ablation. \\textbf{Evaluator baselines.} (a) \\emph{Column mean}: predict each benchmark's history mean. (b) \\emph{Random-$k$ ridge}: five random $k$-subsets (seeded from the dataset, $k$ and draw index), ridge imputer fitted on a rank-5 SVD-completed history (40 completion iterations), ridge strength chosen by leave-one-out over four values; the mean $\\nrmse$ over draws is the baseline. (c) \\emph{Greedy-forward ridge}: forward selection (up to 20 benchmarks) minimising leave-one-out ridge error on the remaining benchmarks of the completed history, with the same ridge imputer. Both ridge baselines share their imputer, so they probe selection with the imputer fixed; the proposed pair changes both selector and imputer.\n\n\\subsection{Selector and imputer}\\label{sec:impl}\n\\paragraph{Representation and EM.} Two representations of the history are built: z-scoring (clipped at $\\pm8$) and an empirical rank-Gauss map $\\Phi^{-1}\\big((r-\\tfrac12)/n\\big)$ per benchmark (average ranks; benchmarks with fewer than eight observations or four distinct values fall back to z-scores). For each, EM fits $\\mathcal{N}(\\mu,\\Sigma)$ with missing entries, applying diagonal shrinkage $\\Sigma\\leftarrow(1-\\epsilon)\\Sigma+\\epsilon\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\Sigma)$ ($\\epsilon=0.02$) in each M-step (at most 60 iterations, tolerance $10^{-4}$, 25-s budget).\n\n\\paragraph{Selection.} Candidate subsets come from (i) covariance-trace greedy A-optimal selection: with $R$ the current posterior covariance, add $j$ maximising $\\sum_{i\\in U,i\\ne j}R_{ij}^2/(R_{jj}+\\lambda)$, then update $R\\leftarrow R-R_{\\cdot j}R_{j\\cdot}/(R_{jj}+\\lambda)$; and (ii) a greedy forward \\emph{wrapper} that adds the benchmark minimising five-fold held-out-model squared error of the conditional-Gaussian predictor, with all candidates scored by batched matrix products and an optional coverage weighting (benchmarks missing for many history models are less informative). For each representation and candidate rule, the subset with the lowest history cross-validation error in raw benchmark units is chosen (up to 250 candidates per step).\n\n\\paragraph{Imputation.} For a new model, the Gaussian conditional mean $\\mu+\\Sigma_{\\cdot S}(\\Sigma_{SS}+\\lambda I)^{-1}(y_S-\\mu_S)$ conditions only on the selected benchmarks that model actually has. Shrinkage, factor rank and $\\lambda$ are tuned by five-fold history cross-validation. The stack uses, per target benchmark, non-negative weights over six base predictors (three Gaussian conditional means at shrinkage 0, 0.15 and 0.3, ridge on the selected benchmarks, and kNN with 5 and 15 neighbours) plus an implicit column-median term, fitted on out-of-fold history predictions and regularised toward a pooled weight vector; a regularisation strength is chosen from six values by cross-validation. Outputs are mapped back to raw units, clipped to the history range, and replaced by the observed value for selected benchmarks; a model with no selected score receives the column median.\n\n\\paragraph{Cost and applicability.} All components are CPU-only NumPy. Fitting plus selection for one $(\\text{dataset},k)$ episode is dominated by EM and cross-validation; the sealed evaluation of 14 episodes per seed took about 157--161 seconds per seed. The method presumes a history matrix with enough coverage per benchmark and a new model whose scores are exchangeable with history models after transformation, which family-disjoint evaluation tests but does not guarantee. Several steps are guarded by wall-clock budgets (candidate evaluation after 70 s, full tuning after 150 s, stacking after 210 s), so on slow hardware the method degrades to the Gaussian-only model.\n\n\\subsection{Experimental protocol}\\label{sec:protocol}\n\\paragraph{Data.} Sealed evaluation uses three public leaderboards at pinned revisions: MTEB results (297 benchmark columns; 124 history and 37 held-out models), the Open LLM Leaderboard table (six benchmarks; 3371 history and 300 held-out models, the held-out set capped at 300), and HELM Lite v1.13.0 core scenarios (ten scenarios; 66 history and 25 held-out models). Models are grouped by normalised name, family key and content hash (union-find); groups, never single models, are assigned to history or held-out, so no family or duplicated record appears on both sides. Held-out groups are drawn in hash order until at least a quarter of rows are held out. A separate MTEB partition with disjoint groups was used for model development (Section~\\ref{sec:search}).\n\n\\paragraph{Episodes.} Each seed runs 14 episodes: MTEB at $k\\in\\{2,5,10,20\\}$; Open LLM Leaderboard at $k\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$; HELM Lite at $k\\in\\{2,3,5\\}$; plus evaluator-owned controls. In a \\emph{permuted} control, held-out models' observed values on the selected benchmarks are cyclically reassigned to other held-out models (MTEB $k{=}10$, Open LLM $k{=}2$, HELM $k{=}3$); in a \\emph{constant} control the observations are replaced by column means (MTEB $k{=}10$). Controls separate genuine cross-model information from the imputer's marginal prior. The seeds (201, 202, 203) reach only the candidate's random state; the held-out sets, baselines and controls are fixed by hashing.\n\n\\paragraph{Why this protocol answers the question.} Four design decisions tie the measurement to the research question. (i) Family-disjoint held-out models make the new model genuinely new: if siblings of a held-out model were in the history, a nearest-neighbour effect could masquerade as cross-benchmark structure. (ii) Committing to $S$ before any new-model score is observed matches the budgeted setting in which the evaluator cannot adapt the run list, and it prevents the selector from peeking at the targets. (iii) Equal weight per held-out model, and errors in units of each benchmark's history spread, stop benchmarks with large numeric ranges or models with many observed cells from dominating the metric. (iv) Evaluator-owned baselines and permutation controls keep the comparison objects outside the control of the method: the method cannot tune its own competitors. The remaining weakness is that the held-out set is fixed, which we return to in Section~\\ref{sec:limits}.\n\n\\paragraph{Aggregation.} Within a leaderboard, values are averaged over seeds, then over its $k$ grid with equal weights; the pooled metric averages the three leaderboards with weight $1/3$ each. The permuted-control gap is the control $\\nrmse$ minus the matched real-episode $\\nrmse$, averaged over seeds and then over leaderboards. A run counts as valid only if all episodes complete with finite predictions, real episodes beat their controls, and pooled skill against column mean and random-$k$ ridge is positive. The sealed evaluation finished with outcome \\emph{qualified}. This statement concerns the evaluated setting; no further qualification diagnostics were retained.\n\n\\section{Results}\\label{sec:results}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/main_results.pdf}\n\\caption{Sealed evaluation on family-disjoint held-out models. (a) MTEB $\\nrmse$ against budget $k$ (mean over three seeds). (b) $\\nrmse$ per leaderboard (OLL: Open LLM Leaderboard) and pooled over the three. (c) Skill against column-mean, greedy-forward ridge and random-$k$ ridge baselines, pooled over leaderboards. No error bars: the retained metrics are seed means, and the three seeds share the same held-out models.}\n\\label{fig:main}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Main results}\nPooled over the three leaderboards, the proposed pair reaches $\\nrmse=0.562$ and a skill of 0.434 against column-mean imputation, that is, a mean relative error reduction of 43.4\\% (Table~\\ref{tab:final}). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_score]]\nThe three per-seed pooled skills are 0.433, 0.427 and 0.433, so the stochasticity of the method's own cross-validation folds moves the headline by less than 0.006. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.evidence.episodes]]\nThis supports H1 in the evaluated setting. Because all three seeds use identical held-out models, this spread does not measure how the result would change with different held-out models, and we report no confidence interval for the pooled values.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Sealed final metrics of the frozen pair (mean over seeds 201--203; hierarchy described in Section~\\ref{sec:protocol}). Lower $\\nrmse$ is better; skill is relative error reduction against the named baseline.}\n\\label{tab:final}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcl}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value & Meaning\\\\\n\\midrule\nPooled $\\nrmse$ & 0.562 & mean over 3 leaderboards\\\\\n\\quad MTEB / HELM / Open LLM & 0.703 / 0.501 / 0.482 & per leaderboard\\\\\n\\quad MTEB $k{=}2/5/10/20$ & 0.787/0.722/0.664/0.638 & error vs.\\ budget\\\\\nSkill vs.\\ column mean & 0.434 & H1\\\\\nSkill vs.\\ greedy-forward ridge & 0.099 & selector+imputer jointly\\\\\nSkill vs.\\ random-$k$ ridge & 0.016 & selector+imputer jointly\\\\\nPermuted-control gap (nRMSE) & 0.678 & control minus real\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_helm]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_oll]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_greedy_ridge]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_random_selection]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_permuted_control_gap]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Error against the budget $k$}\nOn MTEB, $\\nrmse$ falls monotonically from 0.787 at $k{=}2$ to 0.722 at $k{=}5$, 0.664 at $k{=}10$ and 0.638 at $k{=}20$ (Figure~\\ref{fig:main}a). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb_k2]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb_k5]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb_k10]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb_k20]]\nThe mean reduction per additional benchmark shrinks from about 0.022 between $k{=}2$ and $5$, to 0.012 between 5 and 10, to 0.003 between 10 and 20 (differences of the four tabulated means divided by the number of added benchmarks). This supports the diminishing-returns part of H2 on MTEB, with four budgets and no uncertainty estimate for each point. Even at $k{=}20$, i.e.\\ about 7\\% of the 297 columns, the typical per-cell error is still about 0.64 history standard deviations. We cannot say what $k$ is ``enough'': that depends on an error tolerance we did not define, and the curve has no baseline at each $k$ (the retained metrics report baseline skill only pooled over budgets). The curve is measured for one leaderboard; the Open LLM Leaderboard and HELM Lite grids ($k\\le3$ of 6 and $k\\le5$ of 10) were not retained as per-$k$ curves.\n\n\\subsection{Baselines and controls}\n\\paragraph{Control.} The permuted-observation control raises $\\nrmse$ by 0.678 on average over the matched real episodes (Table~\\ref{tab:final}). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_permuted_control_gap]]\nHence the predictions depend on the new model's own scores on the selected benchmarks rather than on a marginal prior alone. The gap is an absolute $\\nrmse$ difference and should be read against the pooled error of 0.562.\n\n\\paragraph{Selection versus imputation.} Skill against random-$k$ ridge is only 0.016: at the pooled level the proposed pair has a mean relative error 1.6\\% lower than five random subsets imputed by a ridge model on a completed history. Skill against greedy-forward ridge is 0.099. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_random_selection]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_greedy_ridge]]\nBoth figures mix selector and imputer, so neither isolates ``which $k$''. The two baselines share an imputer and differ only in selection; since skill against greedy ridge exceeds skill against random-$k$ ridge by 0.083, the greedy-forward baseline had larger error on average than the random-$k$ baseline in these evaluations. This is an inference from two averaged skills (the per-baseline errors were not retained), and it is the only retained evidence about selection alone: under a fixed ridge imputer, a leave-one-out greedy selector did not beat random subsets. For H3 the grounded conclusion is therefore modest. The pair's accuracy over a ridge imputer does not separate into a selector effect and an imputer effect, and the small margin over random-$k$ suggests that on these leaderboards most of the achievable gain over column means is available with a reasonable imputer and an arbitrary subset.\n\n\\subsection{Difficulty across leaderboards}\n$\\nrmse$ is 0.703 on MTEB, 0.501 on HELM Lite and 0.482 on the Open LLM Leaderboard (Figure~\\ref{fig:main}b), so H4 holds in the sense that error differs. It is not a clean difficulty ranking: the leaderboards differ in the number of benchmarks (297, 10, 6), history size (124, 66, 3371 models), $k$ grids, and what $k$ means as a fraction of the columns. MTEB lists embedding models over many task types, which is also a different object from generative language models; none of the three results transfers to the others. % @evidence[[analysis/protocol_facts.json#final]]\n\n\\subsection{Reading the metrics: sensitivity and failure analysis}\n\\paragraph{Skill is a mean of ratios.} Each skill value is computed per episode and then averaged along the hierarchy of Section~\\ref{sec:protocol}, and is clipped to $[-1,1]$. It is therefore not equal to one minus the ratio of pooled errors. If it were, the two ridge-baseline skills would imply that the greedy-forward baseline has an error about $(1-0.016)/(1-0.099)\\approx 1.09$ times that of the random-$k$ baseline. We read this only as indicative of the sign and rough size of the effect. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_random_selection]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_greedy_ridge]]\n\\paragraph{Where the pair could fail.} Three mechanisms can degrade the predictions without being visible in the pooled numbers: (a) benchmarks with few history observations fall back to z-scores or to coarse medians, and the clipped outputs cannot extrapolate beyond the history range, so a new model that outperforms every history model on a benchmark is necessarily under-predicted on that benchmark; (b) selected benchmarks that a new model has not been run on contribute nothing, and the pair then falls back to a prior that conditions on fewer scores (a model with none of the selected scores receives column medians); (c) the wall-clock guards switch off the stack or optional candidates on slow hardware. We did not construct stress tests for (a)--(c), so we cannot report how often or by how much they matter.\n\\paragraph{What a different outcome would have looked like.} A permuted-control gap near zero would have meant that the predictions were driven by the marginal prior; a negative skill against column means would have refuted H1; a flat MTEB curve would have refuted H2. The first two did not occur and the third is contradicted by the monotone curve, so the study could have refuted them. By contrast, H3 and H4 are not tested sharply: the sealed metrics do not isolate selection, and the leaderboards are not matched in size.\n\n\\subsection{Configuration comparison on the model-development partition}\\label{sec:search}\nDuring development, three configurations were scored on a separate MTEB partition (disjoint groups, 280 columns, 131 history and 32 held-out models) with two seeds and $k\\in\\{2,5,10,20\\}$. Table~\\ref{tab:search} keeps these numbers apart from the sealed ones; $\\nrmse$ values are not comparable across the two partitions.\n% @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#01]] @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#02]] @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#03]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Development-partition comparison (MTEB, two seeds). Score is the mean skill against column mean. The seed columns give the two per-seed scores.}\n\\label{tab:search}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nConfiguration & Score & Seeds 11/12 & $\\nrmse$ & vs.\\ random-$k$ & Perm.\\ gap\\\\\n\\midrule\nSimple pair (trace + cond.\\ mean) & 0.220 & 0.220 / 0.220 & 1.067 & 0.100 & 0.522\\\\\nProposed pair (wrapper + stack) & 0.247 & 0.243 / 0.251 & 1.028 & 0.132 & 0.568\\\\\nProposed + latent-skill member & 0.247 & 0.243 / 0.251 & 1.028 & 0.132 & 0.568\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe proposed pair scores 0.247 against 0.220 for the simple pair, with non-overlapping per-seed ranges (0.243--0.251 vs.\\ 0.220), and also shows a larger skill over random-$k$ selection (0.132 vs.\\ 0.100). % @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#02]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#result.top_program_01]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#result.top_program_03]]\nBecause several components change together and the two seeds share one held-out set, this licenses only the statement that the richer configuration was better on that partition; it does not attribute the gain to the transform, the wrapper selector or the stack. The pair extended with a latent-skill member and a latent A-optimal selector produced the same four metrics as the proposed pair to all reported digits. The extension is kept only if it improves cross-validation error, which is consistent with it being inactive, but no diagnostic recording the choice was retained, so the cause is unknown, and we treat this configuration as a null result. The sealed evaluation was run on the best-scoring configuration; the exact tie means the retained scores alone cannot distinguish the two sources (Supplementary Information).\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\\label{sec:limits}\n\\paragraph{What is and is not shown.} The sealed result supports a bounded statement: on family-disjoint held-out models from three pinned leaderboards, a history-fitted selector--imputer pair predicts unrun benchmark scores with a mean relative error reduction (skill) of 0.434 against column means, and the improvement is not an artefact of the imputer's marginals since permuting the observations erases it. The evidence does not show that cross-benchmark structure is \\emph{exploited by selection}; it shows that observed scores help, and that a random subset with a ridge imputer comes within a skill of 0.016 of the pair.\n\n\\paragraph{Joint scoring and missing ablations.} Selector and imputer are scored together. No controlled experiment varied one while holding the other fixed, so the contributions of the rank-Gauss transform, the wrapper selector, the stack and the clipping are unattributed. The development-partition comparison changes many components at once. A fair selector test would pair each selector with each imputer; we did not run it.\n\n\\paragraph{Uncertainty.} Reported values are seed means. The seeds vary only the method's internal randomness (fold assignments), not the held-out models, baselines or controls, so the 0.006 spread across seeds understates sampling uncertainty over models. Held-out sets are small for MTEB (37 models) and HELM Lite (25), and the held-out groups are few (25 and 7). The 0.016 margin over random-$k$ should be viewed as indistinguishable from zero until a resampling-based interval over held-out models is available; we did not compute one, and we make no significance claim.\n\n\\paragraph{Scope.} Three leaderboards, of which two contain generative language models in small numbers of benchmarks (six and ten) and one contains text-embedding models; scales differ (accuracies, win rates, F1). Family-disjoint splitting was defined by name and content heuristics. Selection is non-adaptive; adaptive stopping or sequential selection could change the picture. Missingness in leaderboards is not random (strong models are run on more benchmarks), which violates the usual missing-at-random assumption of EM-based imputation \\citep{Rubin1976Inference}. The run-time guards in Section~\\ref{sec:impl} make behaviour hardware-dependent.\n\n\\paragraph{Unperformed analyses.} We did not run a theory check of the trace objective, a component ablation, per-family error breakdowns, comparisons with low-rank matrix completion or latent-factor imputers as full alternatives, or other leaderboards. These are not claimed.\n\n\\paragraph{Threats to validity.} (1) \\emph{Leakage through naming.} Family-disjointness relies on name normalisation, a family key and a content hash; a model re-released under an unrecognised name could cross the split. (2) \\emph{Metric scale.} Errors are in history standard deviations of each benchmark, which makes a benchmark with a narrow history spread (e.g.\\ one saturated by strong models) look harder to predict than one with a wide spread. (3) \\emph{Heterogeneous coverage.} Leaderboard missingness is related to model strength and recency; selected benchmarks that are well covered in the history may be missing for new models. (4) \\emph{Configuration freezing.} The pair was chosen on the development partition and then evaluated once on the sealed partitions; the sealed numbers are not selected on, but the development partition is from the same MTEB snapshot, so the MTEB result is closer to the development distribution than the other two leaderboards, which were not used to choose among configurations. (5) \\emph{Implementation coupling.} The result is for one concrete implementation, including its tuning grids and wall-clock guards; a different implementation of the same design could behave differently.\n\n\\paragraph{Practical reading.} For an evaluator with a comparable history, a handful of well-covered benchmarks and a reasonable imputer already remove a large part of the column-mean error, further benchmarks buy progressively less, and an unavoidable residual of about 0.64 history standard deviations remains on MTEB at $k{=}20$.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nWe posed the cold-start question of how well $k$ benchmark runs predict the remaining scores of a model from an unseen family, and evaluated a rank-Gauss, EM-Gaussian, wrapper-selected, NNLS-stacked pair on three leaderboards with family-disjoint held-out models. The sealed evaluation completed and qualified. Pooled nRMSE is 0.562 and skill against column means is 0.434, with a clear gap to permuted controls; on MTEB the error decreases from 0.787 to 0.638 as $k$ rises from 2 to 20 with sharply diminishing returns, and error levels differ across leaderboards. The margin over random-$k$ selection with a ridge imputer is only 0.016 and cannot be assigned to selection or imputation, and the retained evidence suggests that a leave-one-out greedy selector can fail to beat random subsets. The main unresolved questions are the isolated value of selection, uncertainty over held-out models, and behaviour beyond these three leaderboards; each needs additional controlled experiments that this study did not perform.\n\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\n\\subsection{Evaluation protocol details}\nTable~\\ref{tab:data} lists the data partitions used by the sealed evaluation, taken from the evaluator's dataset manifests. % @evidence[[analysis/protocol_facts.json#final]] @evidence[[scripts/extract_protocol_facts.py]]\nGroup counts are numbers of family groups after union-find merging. The model-development partition (MTEB, 280 columns, 131 history models, 32 held-out models, revision identical to the sealed MTEB revision) uses a disjoint set of groups. % @evidence[[analysis/protocol_facts.json#search]]\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Sealed evaluation partitions.}\n\\label{tab:data}\n\\small\n\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{4pt}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n\\toprule\nLeaderboard & Revision & Cols. & History & Held-out & Groups (h/o) & $k$ grid\\\\\n\\midrule\nMTEB results & 6dabbcc & 297 & 124 & 37 & 92 / 25 & 2, 5, 10, 20\\\\\nOpen LLM Leaderboard & 9c09a7c & 6 & 3371 & 300 & 2314 / 266 & 1, 2, 3\\\\\nHELM Lite & v1.13.0 & 10 & 66 & 25 & 16 / 7 & 2, 3, 5\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\noindent Score definitions: MTEB uses each task's main score averaged over the entries of the first available split (test, validation, dev, train), taken from the model revision directory with the most task files; Open LLM Leaderboard uses raw scores of IFEval, BBH, MATH Level 5, GPQA, MUSR and MMLU-PRO, dropping rows with a missing score; HELM Lite uses the ten scenario columns of the core-scenarios table, excluding the mean-win-rate column. Held-out MTEB and HELM Lite models may lack some scores; those cells are not scored. Per-seed pooled scores of the sealed evaluation were 0.4330 (seed 201), 0.4273 (202) and 0.4328 (203), taking 157.4, 160.6 and 161.0 seconds for 14 episodes each. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.evidence.episodes]]\n\n\\subsection{Method specification and hyperparameters}\nTable~\\ref{tab:hyper} collects the constants of the pair described in Section~\\ref{sec:impl}; they are read from the entry-point source of the evaluated configuration (\\texttt{src/program.py}: the EM, greedy-selection, wrapper-selection, stacking and prediction routines). % @evidence[[source_evidence/config_proposed/program.py]]\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Constants of the proposed pair.}\n\\label{tab:hyper}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lp{0.5\\textwidth}}\n\\toprule\nComponent & Setting\\\\\n\\midrule\nEM & shrinkage 0.02, $\\le 60$ iterations, tol.\\ $10^{-4}$, jitter $10^{-4}$, 25-s budget\\\\\nCross-validation & 5 folds over history models\\\\\nCovariance shrinkage grid & 0, 0.05, 0.15, 0.3\\\\\nFactor-rank grid & 2, 4, 6, 10, full\\\\\nRidge $\\lambda$ grid (Gaussian conditional mean) & 0.003, 0.03, 0.1, 0.3\\\\\nSelection candidates & 4 trace-greedy settings, 2 wrapper settings (coverage exponent 0 or 1)\\\\\nWrapper & $\\lambda=0.1$, shrinkage 0.05, $\\le 250$ candidates per step\\\\\nStack base predictors & Gaussian conditional means (shrink 0, 0.15, 0.3), ridge ($\\lambda=5$), kNN (5, 15)\\\\\nStack regularisation grid & 0, 0.05, 0.25, 1, 5, $10^{5}$\\\\\nClipping & z-scores at $\\pm 8$; outputs to the history range\\\\\nWall-clock guards & optional candidates 70 s; full tuning 150 s; stacking 210 s\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\noindent The latent-skill extension (third row of Table~\\ref{tab:search}) adds a low-rank model $T_{ij}\\approx g_j(u_i\\cdot v_j+b_j)$ with a monotone piecewise-linear link $g_j$ per benchmark, Huber-likelihood MAP inference of a new model's latent vector with a Laplace covariance, an additional stack member retained only if cross-validation improves, and a latent A-optimal selector candidate. Its metrics equal those of the proposed pair (Table~\\ref{tab:search}); the extension took roughly 331--334 seconds per search seed against 141--142 seconds for the proposed pair. % @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#03]]\nThe longer run time indicates that the extra code ran, but its influence on outputs is not established. The final-evaluation record lists a hash of the evaluated source tree; we were not able to match this hash to a single source directory, and the tie above means the retained scores cannot discriminate the two. The description in the main text is of the shared stack, and the extension is described here for completeness. The simple pair used 95--97 seconds per search seed. % @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#01]]\n\n\\subsection{Extra evidence tables}\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Development-partition per-seed scores and durations (seconds, two seeds each).}\n\\label{tab:seeds}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nConfiguration & Score (seed 11) & Score (seed 12) & Time (seed 11) & Time (seed 12)\\\\\n\\midrule\nSimple pair & 0.2201 & 0.2197 & 95.4 & 97.3\\\\\nProposed pair & 0.2429 & 0.2506 & 140.9 & 142.2\\\\\nProposed + latent member & 0.2429 & 0.2506 & 331.2 & 334.3\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Unperformed analyses}\nThe following were not run and are not claimed: component ablations (the ablation directory of the run is missing); empirical verification of the trace objective as expected error; bootstrap or model-resampling intervals; per-leaderboard $k$ curves for Open LLM Leaderboard and HELM Lite as retained metrics; baseline $\\nrmse$ values per budget; additional leaderboards such as LiveBench, BigCodeBench or AlpacaEval, which the task description lists as optional.\n\n\\subsection{Reproducibility, data and licences}\nEntry point: a single Python file exposing a \\texttt{handle} request function (operations \\texttt{reset}, \\texttt{load\\_history}, \\texttt{select}, \\texttt{predict}); dependencies are NumPy only; behaviour is deterministic given the seed except for the wall-clock guards. Figures and tables in this paper come from \\texttt{scripts/build\\_figures.py} (Figure~\\ref{fig:main}), \\texttt{scripts/extract\\_protocol\\_facts.py} (Table~\\ref{tab:data}), \\texttt{scripts/extract\\_search\\_metrics.py} (Tables~\\ref{tab:search} and~\\ref{tab:seeds}); the sealed metrics are read from the evaluation registry. Data sources are public: MTEB results (CC0-1.0), Open LLM Leaderboard contents (no dataset licence declared on its card), and HELM Lite release v1.13.0 (scenario data under per-scenario licences, not individually verified). Literature retrieval used only titles from the starter literature and standard methodology references; no material from the excluded publication was knowingly accessed.\n\n\\subsection{LLM Usage}\nLarge language models were used for research hypothesis generation, for implementing and running the compared configurations, and for drafting and editing this manuscript. The selector--imputer designs, code, experiments and the text were produced by an automated LLM-driven research workflow, with evaluator-owned scoring that the workflow could not modify. All reported numbers were read from evaluator outputs and checked against the evidence registry; the human role was limited to specifying the task and evaluation setting. References were retrieved from bibliographic databases and checked against their records.\n\n\\end{document}\n","numLines":299,"startLine":1,"totalLines":299}}}
